Chapter: CiteOverrideAlpha

 Two chapters define cite-shared, so the name is contested and CDX3006
 reports it at both sites. A third chapter defines neither and cites one
 of them by name, which is the documented way to say which definition a
 mention means.

 The cite here names the QUIRE, not the directory the chapter sits in.
 That spelling is the whole point of this test. The concat prefixes each
 header with its DIRECTORY, so a cite written against the quire could
 never match a key composed as <Quire>--<Chapter>, and the override was
 silently dropped: find-slug-for-cite-name answered "" and the mention
 fell through to whichever definition the glob order happened to supply.
 Twenty-one of the compiler's own cites are written this way.

 The runtime answer is the claim. CiteOverrideMain defines no
 cite-shared and cites Alpha's, so it must answer 2.

 What the old key did here is worth stating exactly, because it is not
 what the backlog entry predicted. It did not pick the wrong definition
 silently. It could not compile this file AT ALL: both definitions are
 contested and therefore mangled, the citing chapter got no rename entry
 because the lookup answered "", and the mention resolved to nothing --
 `error CDX3002: Undefined name: cite-shared`, no binary emitted. The
 failure was loud, which is why nothing in the tree had hit it: every
 cite written this way happens to name a definition that is not
 contested. So this test does not pin a wrong answer, it pins a
 construct that could not be compiled before and can be now.

Section: Alpha
  cite-shared : Integer -> Integer
  cite-shared (n) = n + 1

Chapter: CiteOverrideBeta

 The other half of the contested pair.

Section: Beta
  cite-shared : Integer -> Integer
  cite-shared (n) = n + 100

Chapter: CiteOverrideMain
  cites Codex chapter CiteOverrideAlpha (cite-shared)

 Defines neither definition and names the one it means.

Section: Entry
  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni ("cite-shared 1 = " & show (cite-shared 1))
  end
